234PU c  2004Sa05: {+233}U({+6}Li,5n), E=39-48 MeV on the targets; A stack of   
234PU2c  21 {+233}UO{-2}(NO{-3}){-2} targets (50-130 |mg/cm{+2} on 0.9          
234PU3c  mg/cm{+2} aluminum backing) was bombarded with a 51.0 MeV {+6}Li beam  
234PU4c  at 250 to 340 particle nA from the JAERI tandem. The beam through a    
234PU5c  Havar vacuum window and helium cooling gas before reaching the         
234PU6c  targets. Reaction products recoiling out of the targets were stopped   
234PU7c  by He gas (94 kPa) loaded with PbI{-2} aerosol clusters. The products  
234PU8c  attached to the clusters were continuously swept out of the target     
234PU9c  chamber with a He gas flow and transported to a thermal ion source of  
234PUac  the Isotope Separater On Line (ISOL). After being ionized in the ion   
234PUbc  source, the products were accelerated with 30 kV and mass separated    
234PUcc  using the ISOL. The mass-separated ions were implanted into foils      
234PUdc  placed on the rotating wheel. The implanted sources were carried by    
234PUec  the wheel to detector stations, where |a particles were measured with  
234PUfc  Si detectors (PIN photodiode) and characteristic x rays were measured  
234PUgc  with a coaxial Ge detector. Production of {+234}Am was confirmed by    
234PUhc  observation of Pu k|a1 x-rays and T{-1/2} was determined from the      
234PUic  decay curve. 2002As08 and 2003Na10: earlier reports by the same group. 
234PU c  1990Ha02,1989Ha37: {+237}Np({|a,7n){+234}Am, E=70-73.5 MeV on the      
234PU2c  targets; Chemically purified {+237}Np (150-250 |mg) was                
234PU3c  electrodeposited on 25 |mm Be foils. A stack of the 15 {+237}Np        
234PU4c  targets (125 to 200 |mg/cm{+2} each; about a cm spacing between each   
234PU5c  target) was bombarded with 75 MeV |a beam at 3-6 particle |mA. The     
234PU6c  recoil products were collected on KCl aerosols in helium inside the    
234PU7c  target chamber, which swept out the volume behind each target          
234PU8c  continuously. The aerosols were transported to the rotating wheel      
234PU9c  system or to a chemistry laboratory. On-line measurements of fission   
234PUac  fragments and |a were made with a pair of ion-implanted Si detectors   
234PUbc  using the aerosols implanted on the rotating wheel system. T{-1/2}     
